          "question": "Since my knee replacement I have had calf pain and the leg looks swollen. How urgent is that, and what does guidance describe about the risk?",
          "answer": "**Please contact a clinician today about the calf pain and swelling.** The automated urgency check rated this as routine, but it only matches a fixed list of patterns and says itself that it is not a clearance. Calf pain and swelling after a knee replacement are a recognised reason to check for a blood clot in the leg, but the sources I could search don't confirm this or say how serious your case is. Only a clinician can assess that.\n\n**Call emergency services now if you have any of these:**\n- Chest pain. The chest pain guidance (red-flag-chest-pain) lists pulmonary embolism, a clot that travels to the lungs, among the immediately life-threatening causes.\n- Breathlessness, especially at rest.\n- Fainting, collapse, or feeling faint.\n- A racing heartbeat, or coughing up blood. The passages I retrieved don't cover coughing up blood, but it is a reason to call emergency services immediately.\n\nDo not wait to see whether these settle.\n\n**Contact your surgical team or a doctor today** if the calf pain or swelling is new, worsening, or not settling, even if none of the above applies. Tell them about the knee replacement and when the symptoms started. An urgent care service is another option.\n\n**On how common clots are, what prevention is advised, and where experts disagree:** the guidance I searched says nothing about blood clots after knee replacement. The disagreements that came up concern other conditions, such as knee injections, cholesterol targets and diabetes screening, so they don't apply to your question. Your surgical team can explain your personal risk and what they are monitoring for.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
